{"author":"rebar-bridge[bot]","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"compacted_at":1789685376716311865,"compiled_state":{"alias":"peaceable-chunky-harvestmen","assignee":null,"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","authorship":{"signed":2,"unsigned":0},"authorship_ledger":[{"content_hash":"e5f779516ee0eb15172219ae77d9c22b0b56cb6738ba5acad921c1b1f972b657","event_uuid":"505a0bd8-977a-48a5-9c85-135cf411ea9b","position":{"commit_sha":"3c5542b79fca9ec212af63062d2da5d3b19c650a","position":"1789669835710639806-505a0bd8-977a-48a5-9c85-135cf411ea9b"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":null},{"content_hash":"eb53f55189195fb4b34db81e40641111eb3276acaff0a1797551167fb20a7fed","event_uuid":"96292ca0-b621-41d5-bc16-4cf5b38d7bbe","position":{"commit_sha":"1e278c73955120bf07115e4e4c1da8572ee3c4dd","position":"1789685376716311864-96292ca0-b621-41d5-bc16-4cf5b38d7bbe"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":null}],"bridge_alerts":[],"bridge_project":null,"claim_harness":null,"claim_remote_session":null,"claimed_session":null,"comments":[{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"Read-only investigation complete. The requested split was performed and it refutes the ticket's own premise in two ways.\n\n## Correction: the population is 4, not ~52\n\nThe ticket is framed around \"~52 failures\" of a single kind. That conflated distinct causes. Against unmodified `origin/main` in the sandbox, `make test` produced **51 total failures, of which only 4 are SemLock**. The other 47 are separate, unrelated sandbox problems — `pypandoc`/pandoc execution, `ps` denial, and git identity assumptions among them. They were not root-caused, since this ticket's requested split is SemLock-specific, but they must not keep being counted as part of this defect.\n\n## The split: runner-level is zero\n\n| Population | Count | Modules |\n|---|---:|---|\n| Runner-level pytest-xdist | **0** | none |\n| Test-level multiprocessing | **4** | `tests/unit/test_network_guard_local_ipc.py`, `tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_atomic_completion_close.py`, `tests/unit/test_marker_fcntl_guard.py`, `tests/scripts/graph/test_doctor_locks.py` |\n\nxdist is **not** affected. `pytest -n 4 --dist worksteal` brought its worker pool up successfully and reached 99%; no SemLock traceback originated from xdist startup or coordination. The 4 test-level failures persisted under `pytest -p no:xdist`, and those 4 exact nodeids fail in 41.56s standalone — proving they are not runner-level parallelism.\n\nThis matters for the remedy: **changing the worker count cannot fix anything**, because the runner-level population it would address is empty.\n\n## Confirmed root cause\n\nThe sandbox denies Darwin named POSIX semaphore creation (`sem_open`) with `EPERM`, and CPython `multiprocessing` has no usable fallback on this host because `sem_init` returns `ENOSYS`.\n\nA standalone C probe outside pytest:\n\n```\nsem_open(/mp38223probe, O_CREAT|O_EXCL, 0600, 1) failed errno=1 Operation not permitted\nsem_init failed errno=78 Function not implemented\n```\n\nDirect `_multiprocessing.SemLock(...)` calls failed identically for every legal name shape tested, while ordinary file creation in the worktree succeeded — so this is a denial of the semaphore syscall itself, not of a backing-file path.\n\nRepresentative traceback:\n\n```text\nFAILED tests/unit/test_network_guard_local_ipc.py::test_network_guard_allows_forkserver_multiprocessing\n    ctx = mp.get_context(\"forkserver\")\n>   ready = ctx.Event()\nmultiprocessing/synchronize.py:57: in __init__\n    sl = self._semlock = _multiprocessing.SemLock(\nE   PermissionError: [Errno 1] Operation not permitted\n```\n\nNo start method avoids it. `spawn`, `fork`, and `forkserver` all fail constructing `Lock`, `RLock`, `Semaphore`, `BoundedSemaphore`, `Event`, `Queue`, and `Pool`. `ThreadPoolExecutor` works but is not a process-isolation substitute. `multiprocessing.dummy.Pool` also fails, because in this Python it still constructs a multiprocessing `SimpleQueue`.\n\n## `PYTEST_WORKERS=1` is not the answer\n\nIt does not even bypass xdist: `make test` hardcodes `-n $(PYTEST_WORKERS) --dist worksteal`, so `PYTEST_WORKERS=1` runs `-n 1`, still under xdist. The run passed lint, typecheck and import-convention, then logged failures by 3% of the suite. It was stopped and reaped at 42.61 minutes while still at 19%.\n\nFull single-worker wall-clock is therefore **unverified** — the run did not complete. It was terminated because it had already failed and completing several more hours could not make it a green gate, not because a runtime measurement was obtained.\n\n## Existing handling\n\n`Makefile`: `PYTEST_WORKERS ?= 4`, `verify: lint typecheck import-convention test`, and `test` hardcodes the xdist invocation above. `tests/conftest.py` already sandboxes `REBAR_ROOT` and gate temp dirs, and its network guard already permits AF_UNIX for forkserver IPC — but there is **no** POSIX semaphore capability probe and no SemLock or sandbox handling anywhere.\n\n## Fix direction\n\nNot the worker count. Add a direct **capability probe** — attempt `SemLock` / `sem_open` and observe the result, satisfying the no-environment-heuristics constraint — and handle only the 4 affected tests explicitly. Prefer rewriting a test to avoid `multiprocessing` wherever process fidelity is genuinely preserved; where it is not, mark it as requiring POSIX named-semaphore capability and emit a **loud, counted** signal on the sandbox path, never a silent skip. CI's `-n 4` full coverage stays unchanged.\n\n## The gate is still not restored by this\n\nStating this plainly because it bears on the ticket's escalation clause: fixing all 4 SemLock failures does **not** make `make verify` green in the sandbox. The other 47 failures remain, from at least three further capability denials. This ticket, as scoped, removes one of several blockers. Restoring `make verify` as a local gate under the sandbox requires the remaining 47 to be triaged as well, and that is not in scope here. A follow-up ticket for that triage has a clean boundary and would let this one close on its own terms.\n\nNo code changes were made.","timestamp":1789685376716311864}],"created_at":1789669835710639806,"creation_channel":"mcp","deps":[],"description":"## Problem\n\n`make verify` is the stated pre-push contract in `AGENTS.md`: it is the one local command that lets an author say \"I verified this\" and be right, and it is the locally checkable half of the `Verified` CI vote. On an agent workstation running under the OS-level filesystem/IPC sandbox, it **cannot produce a verdict at all**.\n\nRoughly 52 errors are raised, all with the same signature:\n\n```\n_multiprocessing.SemLock -> PermissionError: [Errno 1] Operation not permitted\n```\n\nThe sandbox refuses POSIX semaphore creation. This was proven environmental rather than change-induced by running the identical command against an unmodified `origin/main` baseline and observing the same failures. `make lint` and `make typecheck` pass in the same environment, so the breakage is confined to the test-execution half of `verify`.\n\n## Why this matters\n\nThe consequence is not slow tests — it is a **missing gate**. Agents working in this environment currently cannot run the contract at all, so every change is pushed on `lint` + `typecheck` + targeted `pytest` and relies on CI's `Verified` vote to catch the large family of invariants that this repo enforces through pytest rather than through lint scripts: the ratchets' baseline-vs-tree comparisons, the public-API surface census, CI-workflow parity, generated-artifact and docs drift, and the whole-tree AST policy scans. `AGENTS.md` documents an incident on 2026-09-04 in which three changes were pushed red by exactly those tests after their authors saw `lint` and `typecheck` green. That is precisely the failure mode this environment now forces on every agent by default.\n\nA pre-push contract that cannot run in the environment its users work in is not a contract.\n\n## Required outcome\n\n`make verify` must produce a trustworthy verdict in **both** environments:\n\n- **CI** — unchanged. It must keep selecting exactly what the gating `ubuntu-latest, py3.13` cell selects (`not integration and not external`), with no reduction in what is executed and no new ability to pass while tests are skipped.\n- **Local sandboxed workstation** — it must run to completion and report a real pass/fail, rather than aborting on an environment capability it does not actually need.\n\n## Investigation required first\n\nDo not adjust the runner before establishing which component requests the semaphore. Candidates to distinguish:\n\n1. The parallel test runner itself (`make test` defaults to `PYTEST_WORKERS=4`), where parallelism is an execution detail and serial execution would preserve the selection contract exactly.\n2. Individual tests or fixtures that use `multiprocessing` / `ProcessPoolExecutor` directly, where the capability is part of what is under test and silently skipping them WOULD weaken the contract.\n\nThese two have materially different correct fixes, and the 52 failures may be a mix of both. Identify the actual distribution before proposing anything.\n\n## Constraints\n\n- Degrading the local run must never silently reduce coverage. If any test genuinely requires a capability the sandbox denies, that must be reported explicitly in the run's output and summary — a verdict that hides skipped tests is worse than the current hard failure, because it reads as success.\n- CI behaviour must not be weakened to accommodate the sandbox. Any fallback must be conditional on detecting the denied capability, not on a hand-set flag that could drift on in CI.\n- Detection must be a real capability probe, not a hostname, username, or environment-name guess.\n- The mechanism-delta ratchet is shrink-only and fails on any new unadmitted mechanism. If this fix needs a new env var or config key, it must be justified at its definition site with the documented `mechanism-ok` marker, or avoided.\n\n## Escalation\n\nIf a solution meeting all of the above is not achievable while following sound CI and DevOps practice — in particular if the only options are to weaken the CI contract or to let local runs report success while skipping tests — do NOT pick the lesser evil. Stop and escalate to the operator with the tradeoff stated plainly.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] The component(s) requesting POSIX semaphores are identified, with the failure count attributed between runner-level parallelism and test-level use of multiprocessing.\n- [ ] `make verify` runs to completion and produces a pass/fail verdict on a sandboxed local workstation.\n- [ ] CI continues to select and execute exactly what it selects today; no test that runs in CI today is skipped there after this change.\n- [ ] Any locally unrunnable test is reported explicitly in the local run's output and summary rather than silently skipped.\n- [ ] Capability detection is a genuine runtime probe, not an environment-name heuristic.\n- [ ] `docs/local-dev-env.md` documents the local behaviour and how it differs from CI.\n- [ ] The change adds no unadmitted mechanism, or justifies any addition at its definition site.\n","env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","file_impact":[],"file_impact_scope":"undeclared","keyring":[],"managed_refs":[],"no_file_impact_reason":"","parent_id":null,"parent_status_uuid":"","plan_review_phase":"planning","preconditions_summary":{"status":"pre-manifest"},"priority":1,"repos":[],"reverts":[],"status":"open","tags":[],"ticket_id":"0eba-cfd8-34b0-4cb7","ticket_type":"bug","title":"make verify cannot run under the agent sandbox: POSIX semaphore denial aborts the pre-push contract","verify_commands":[]},"source_event_uuids":["505a0bd8-977a-48a5-9c85-135cf411ea9b","96292ca0-b621-41d5-bc16-4cf5b38d7bbe"]},"env_id":"","event_type":"SNAPSHOT","timestamp":1789685376716311865,"uuid":"d0114c9f-fbc7-49f4-af10-74e726ecd307"}